                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="277-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="277-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="277-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_hc.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Hypercardioid" title="Hypercardioid" ></span>AKG Acoustics C 4000 B</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>AKG&#8217;s C4000B is a large-diaphragm multipattern FET condenser with an electret capsule.</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/akg/C4000B-capsule.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/akg/C4000B-capsule-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="AKG C4000B Capsule" width="133" /></a>The capsule has two 1-inch (25mm) diaphragms, in a shockmounted capsule assembly.</p><p>A switch on the microphone body allows selection of Cardioid, Hypercardioid, or Omni pickup patterns.</p><p>The microphone&#8217;s self-noise rating is very low, 8dBA.</p><p>A switchable 10-dB preattenuation pad allows the C 4000 B to be used for close-in miking of instruments producing extremely high sound pressure levels such as guitar amps etc. A switchable bass cut filter eliminates low-frequency noise.</p><p>The C4000B ships with an elastic shockmout (AKG p/n H 85), a flight case, and a foam windscreen.</p><p><span style="font-family: Verdana,Sans-Serif; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; font-size: 10px; line-height: normal; font-size-adjust: none; font-stretch: normal; -x-system-font: none;">Capsule photo credit: Markus Sauschlager</span><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/AKG-Acoustics/C-4000-B">AKG Acoustics C 4000 B</a> is also known as: C4000B, C 4000 B.</p>
